### Problem 2: Dog [[class|class]]
> **Write a java program that creates a java [[class|class]] named "Dog". Where, the [[class|class]] attributes are breed, age, and color. The [[class|class]] methods are `setBreed()`, `setColor()`, and `printDetails()` to assign values of the dog breed, age, color and display these attributes respectively. Create another [[class|class]] named "TestDog", and create an [[object|object]] named `obj` of Dog [[class|class]] to access its methods.**

**Solution:**
```java
// Dog.java
[[public|public]] [[class|class]] Dog {
    // Attributes ([[private|private]] = [[encapsulation|encapsulation]])
    [[private|private]] String breed;
    [[private|private]] int age;
    [[private|private]] String color;

    // Setters
    [[public|public]] void setBreed(String breed) {
        [[this|this]].breed = breed;
    }

    [[public|public]] void setAge(int age) {
        if (age > 0) [[this|this]].age = age;
    }

    [[public|public]] void setColor(String color) {
        [[this|this]].color = color;
    }

    // Print details
    [[public|public]] void printDetails() {
        System.out.println("Breed: " + breed);
        System.out.println("Age: " + age);
        System.out.println("Color: " + color);
    }
}

// TestDog.java
[[public|public]] [[class|class]] TestDog {
    [[public|public]] static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog obj = new Dog();  // Create [[object|object]]
        obj.setBreed("Labrador");
        obj.setAge(3);
        obj.setColor("Golden");
        obj.printDetails();  // Display
    }
}
```

**[[output|output]]:**
```
Breed: Labrador
Age: 3
Color: Golden
```

