### Problem 3: Properties of Java Classes
> **What are the properties of Java Classes?**

**Answer:**
1. **[[encapsulation|encapsulation]]** — [[data-hiding|data hiding]] via access modifiers
2. **[[inheritance|inheritance]]** — reuse via `[[extends|extends]]`
3. **[[polymorphism|polymorphism]]** — one [[interface|interface]], multiple implementations
4. **[[abstraction|abstraction]]** — hide complexity, show essentials
5. **Instantiation** — create objects with `new`
6. **Members** — fields, methods, constructors, nested classes
7. **Modifiers** — `[[public|public]]`, `abstract`, `[[final|final]]`, `strictfp`
8. **Interfaces** — `[[implements|implements]]` multiple interfaces

---

## Section 7 — Self-Check Questions

1. What is the default [[access-modifier|access modifier]] (package-[[private|private]])?
2. Can a [[class|class]] have multiple constructors?
3. What happens if you define a [[constructor|constructor]] but want the default no-arg too?
4. What does `[[this|this]]()` do in a [[constructor|constructor]]?
5. Difference between [[static-[[field|field]]|static [[field|field]]]] and [[instance-[[field|field]]|instance [[field|field]]]]?
6. Why make fields `[[private|private]]`?

---

## Solutions

1. Package-[[private|private]] (no [[keyword|keyword]]) — accessible within same package
2. Yes — [[[[constructor|constructor]]-overloading|[[constructor|constructor]] overloading]]
3. Must explicitly define it — Java won't provide default if any [[constructor|constructor]] exists
4. Calls another [[constructor|constructor]] of same [[class|class]] (must be first statement)
5. Static = one per [[class|class]] (shared); Instance = one per [[object|object]]
6. [[encapsulation|encapsulation]] — control access, validate, hide implementation

---

