### Problem 2: [[encapsulation|encapsulation]] Explanation + Program
> **Explain how [[encapsulation|encapsulation]] is achieved in [[object|object]]-oriented programming, and write a program to support your explanation.**

**Explanation:**
[[encapsulation|encapsulation]] is achieved by:
1. Making fields **[[private|private]]** — hidden from outside
2. Providing **[[public|public]] getters/setters** — controlled access
3. **Validating** in setters — prevent invalid [[state|state]]

**Program:**
```java
[[public|public]] [[class|class]] Student {
    [[private|private]] String name;
    [[private|private]] int age;
    [[private|private]] double gpa;

    [[public|public]] String getName() { return name; }
    [[public|public]] void setName(String name) {
        if (name != null && !name.isBlank()) [[this|this]].name = name;
    }

    [[public|public]] int getAge() { return age; }
    [[public|public]] void setAge(int age) {
        if (age >= 16 && age <= 100) [[this|this]].age = age;
    }

    [[public|public]] double getGpa() { return gpa; }
    [[public|public]] void setGpa(double gpa) {
        if (gpa >= 0.0 && gpa <= 5.0) [[this|this]].gpa = gpa;
    }
}

[[public|public]] [[class|class]] TestEncapsulation {
    [[public|public]] static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student();
        s.setName("Alice");
        s.setAge(20);
        s.setGpa(4.5);
        System.out.println(s.getName() + ", " + s.getAge() + ", " + s.getGpa());

        s.setAge(-5);  // Ignored ([[validation|validation]])
        s.setGpa(6.0); // Ignored ([[validation|validation]])
        System.out.println(s.getAge() + ", " + s.getGpa());  // 20, 4.5
    }
}
```

